# source: Economic Input/Resources/Enrolled Agents/see3-sample-test-questions-2024.pdf
# format: line_number<TAB>text
1	SEE Sample Test Questions Part 3
2	1. Under Treasury Department Circular No. 230, which of the following is incompetence or disreputable
3	conduct?
4	A. Conviction of any misdemeanor offense under Federal, State, or local law
5	B. Conviction of any criminal offense involving dishonesty or breach of trust
6	C. Willfully disclosing a taxpayer’s tax return information with the consent of the taxpayer
7	D. Due to reasonable cause, forgetting to sign a tax return prepared by the practitioner when the
8	practitioner’s signature is required by Federal tax laws
9	Key: B
10	References: Cir. 230, Section 10.51
11	2. Which of the following from Circular 230 is correct regarding sanctions against an enrolled agent (EA)?
12	A. There cannot be both monetary and nonmonetary sanctions imposed
13	B. An EA can face Imprisonment up to 3 years
14	C. An EA can be censured
15	D. A monetary penalty can exceed the gross income derived from the conduct giving rise to the penalty
16	Key: C
17	References: Cir. 230, Section 10.50
18	3. How many future years will the IRS record to the Centralized Authentication File (CAF) at the
19	taxpayer’s request based on receipt of a valid Form 2848 authorizing representation for tax years or
20	periods?
21	A. December 31 of year of receipt + 1 year
22	B. December 31 of year of receipt + 2 years
23	C. December 31 of year of receipt + 3 years
24	D. December 31 of year of receipt + 4 years
25	Key: C
26	References: 26 C.F.R. § 601.506(d)(3)(ii); Instructions for Form 2848, Power of Attorney and Declaration
27	of Representative
28	4. A taxpayer filed an appeal of an IRS examination and then signed Form 8821, Tax Information
29	Authorization, authorizing an enrolled agent (EA) to receive the taxpayer’s confidential tax information
30	related to the matter before the Independent Office of Appeals. Which of the following is correct
31	regarding the EA’s ability to represent the taxpayer regarding the appeal?
32	A. The EA may represent the client based on the Form 8821
33	B. The EA may represent the client with the oral consent of the client
34	C. The EA may represent the client with a note attached to Form 8821
35	D. The EA may not represent the client based on the Form 8821
36	Key: D
37	References: IRM 21.3.7.5; Publication 947, Practice Before the IRS and Power of Attorney; Form 8821
38	Instructions
39	
40	5. To meet due diligence requirements, which of the following is true regarding the Earned Income
41	Credit Worksheet and Form 8867?
42	A. A record must be kept of how, when, and from whom the information used to prepare the Form
43	8867 and the Worksheets was obtained
44	B. Form 8867 does not need to be filed with the return if all information used to complete the form was
45	provided by the taxpayer in writing
46	C. A record of any questions the taxpayer may have asked the tax preparer about their eligibility for the
47	credits should be retained
48	D. Copies of documents provided by the taxpayer that the return preparer relied on to determine the
49	eligibility for the credits do not need to be retained by the preparer
50	Key: A
51	References: Treas. Reg. § 1.6695-2(b); Instructions for Form 8867
52	6. An enrolled agent (EA) is in the process of representing a taxpayer before the Internal Revenue
53	Service for a tax matter involving a tax return the taxpayer filed claiming married filing jointly with her
54	former husband. The taxpayer's former husband also asked the EA to represent him for the same
55	matter. Which of the following is true regarding the EA representing them both?
56	A. The EA must notify the Office of Professional Responsibility that the EA will be representing both
57	taxpayers
58	B. Each taxpayer only needs to provide consent verbally, if the EA documents the day consent was given
59	C. The EA must reasonably believe that the EA will be able to provide competent and diligent
60	representation to both taxpayers
61	D. Confirmation of a conflict of interest must be made within 60 days from the time of consent by both
62	taxpayers
63	Key: C
64	References: Cir. 230, Section 10.29
65	7. An enrolled agent prepared an individual income tax return for a taxpayer with a balance due of
66	$25,597. The taxpayer is not able to pay the entire amount upon filing and would like to set up an
67	installment agreement. Which of the following statements are correct about this agreement?
68	A. Since the taxpayer owed more than $25,000 the taxpayer may not apply online
69	B. The taxpayer will not be charged a user fee to set up this installment agreement
70	C. The taxpayer must be in filing compliance
71	D. The taxpayer will not be charged interest and penalties while making installment payments
72	Key: C
73	References: Publication 594, The IRS Collection Process; IRM. 5.14.1.4.2(4)
74	8. For the IRS to grant a guaranteed installment agreement, a taxpayer must have not failed to file any
75	income tax returns or pay any tax shown on such returns during any of the preceding:
76	A. 3 taxable years
77	B. 5 taxable years
78	C. 6 taxable years
79	D. 10 taxable years
80	Key: B
81	References: IRC § 6159(c)(2)
82	
83	9. The IRS may accept an Offer in Compromise filed by the taxpayer or their representative under which
84	of the conditions below?
85	A. The taxpayer’s tax debt might not be accurate
86	B. All the taxpayer’s liabilities have been referred to the Department of Justice for prosecution or
87	defense
88	C. The taxpayer has filed Chapter 13 bankruptcy
89	D. The taxpayer failed to file all legally required tax returns before the offer is submitted
90	Key: A
91	References: Publication 594, The IRS Collection Process; I.R.C. § 7122(a); Treas. Reg. § 301.7122-1(b)(1)
92	10. Which of the following tasks may be performed by an enrolled agent on behalf of his or her client?
93	A. Prepare and file a suit for refund in United States District Court
94	B. Prepare and sign a United States Tax Court petition to contest a notice of deficiency
95	C. Prepare and sign a protest to challenge examination results in the IRS Independent Office of Appeals
96	D. Prepare and file a bankruptcy petition in United States Bankruptcy Court due to unpaid tax balances
97	Key: C
98	References: Cir. 230, Section 10.2(a)(4), 10.3(c), and 10.32; Publication 947, Practice Before the IRS and
99	Power of Attorney
100	11. An enrolled agent (EA) may represent a taxpayer:
101	A. At conferences or meetings with the IRS Examination Division
102	B. Only if the EA prepared the return under examination
103	C. At all tax-related federal court proceedings
104	D. Before the Taxpayer Advocate Service and the Tax Court
105	Key: A
106	References: Cir. 230, Sections 10.2(a)(4), 10.3(c) & 10.32; Publication 947, Practice Before the IRS and
107	Power of Attorney
108	12. A new client visits an enrolled agent (EA). The taxpayer believes that the U.S. tax system is purely
109	voluntary and filed a return showing no income tax, requesting all withholding be refunded. The IRS
110	assessed a $5,000 frivolous return penalty.
111	The taxpayer received a Notice of Intent to Levy and Right to Collection Due Process (CDP) hearing
112	concerning the $5,000 penalty. The taxpayer wants the EA to present the previous arguments about the
113	tax system in the CDP hearing request.
114	Which of the following is a correct statement regarding the CDP hearing request raising arguments
115	previously deemed frivolous?
116	A. Filing the CDP request will suspend any levies while the IRS Independent Office of Appeals considers
117	the request
118	B. The EA would not be subject to a frivolous submission penalty by submitting the CDP hearing request
119	C. Since a $5,000 frivolous return penalty has been assessed, a second penalty cannot be assessed for
120	the same tax period
121	D. If the IRS provides the taxpayer with notice that the CDP request is frivolous, the taxpayer will be
122	given 30 days to withdraw or amend the CDP request to avoid a frivolous submission penalty
123	
124	Key: D
125	References: IRC §§ 6330(g), 6702(b)(3)
126	13. A taxpayer received a notice from the IRS stating a prior year’s tax return had been examined and
127	that an adjustment was made increasing the amount of tax by $2,560. The taxpayer disagrees with the
128	adjustment. Under which situation could the taxpayer request an audit reconsideration?
129	A. The full amount owed has already been paid
130	B. The taxpayer has not discovered any new documentation for IRS Exam to consider
131	C. The taxpayer did not appear for the examination or did not send information to the IRS
132	D. The taxpayer moved but received the examination notice
133	Key: C
134	References: IRM 4.13.1.3; IRM 4.13.1.4; Publication 3598, What You Should Know About the Audit
135	Reconsideration Process
136	14. An enrolled agent's (EA's) client is an individual taxpayer who is requesting assistance with a
137	proposed penalty. Which of the following is a method of contesting the penalty?
138	A. Prior to a penalty’s assessment and before any request for relief has been denied, or a 30-day letter
139	has been issued, the EA can request a conference with the IRS Independent Office of Appeals
140	B. Prior to a penalty’s assessment, the EA can request binding arbitration to reconsider the penalty
141	C. Before the penalty has been assessed, the EA can submit a written request for penalty abatement
142	D. After the penalty has been assessed and paid, the EA can file a claim for refund with the IRS
143	Key: D
144	References: Methods of Appealing Penalties, IRM 20.1.1.4(2)
145	15. An enrolled agent’s written communications with a client may be privileged if they concern:
146	A. A criminal tax investigation
147	B. Preparing the client’s tax return
148	C. Representing the client in an IRS examination
149	D. Encouraging the client to participate in a tax shelter
150	Key: C
151	References: IRC § 7525 (a)(1)-(2), (b); United States v. KPMG LLP, 237 F. Supp. 2d 35, 39 (D. D.C. 2002)
152	(Section 7525 “does not protect communications between a tax practitioner and a client simply for the
153	preparation of a tax return”); Publication 556 Examination of Returns, Appeal Rights, and Claims for
154	Refund
155	16. When advertising their services, an enrolled agent may use which one of the following phrases to
156	describe their professional designation?
157	A. Certified to practice before the Internal Revenue Service
158	B. Admitted to practice before the Internal Revenue Service
159	C. Enrolled as a representative of the Internal Revenue Service
160	D. Licensed to practice before the Internal Revenue Service
161	Key: B
162	References: Cir. 230, section 10.30(a)(1)
163	
164	17. Which of the below statements is correct regarding complaints for the sanctioning of a practitioner,
165	employer, firm, appraiser, or other entity for violations of the regulations governing practice before the
166	Internal Revenue Service?
167	A. A United States Tax Court judge oversees proceedings regarding the complaint
168	B. In general, discovery may be permitted at the discretion of an Administrative Law Judge
169	C. Within 30 days of receipt of the answer, the presiding judge will notify the parties of the right to
170	request discovery and the timeframe for filing a request
171	D. The complaint can only be served on the respondent in person by a designated employee of the
172	Internal Revenue Service
173	Key: B
174	References: Cir. 230, Sections 10.63(a), 10.70(a) and 10.71(a)
175	18. If a taxpayer and the IRS fail to settle a non-docketed examination controversy in the IRS
176	Independent Office of Appeals, the next event to occur is:
177	A. Issuance of a notice of deficiency
178	B. Issuance of notice and demand for payment
179	C. Referral of the case to the Taxpayer Advocate Service
180	D. Return of the case to the assigned Revenue Agent for further review
181	Key: A
182	References: ; Treas. Reg. 601.106(d)(2)(ii); Publication 556, Examination of Returns, Appeal Rights, and
183	Claims for Refund
184	19. To avoid imposition of the accuracy-related penalty for a substantial understatement of income tax,
185	which of the following is a type of authority that may be relied upon by the taxpayer to try to show
186	substantial authority for the tax treatment of an item?
187	A. www.irs.gov
188	B. Treasury regulations
189	C. IRS Forms
190	D. IRS Publications
191	Key: B
192	References: Treas. Reg. § 1.6662-4(d)(3)(iii); Publication 17, Your Federal Income Tax (For Individuals)
193	20. Which of the following statements is true regarding rejected electronic filed returns?
194	A. The ERO must notify the taxpayer within 48 hours if they can’t fix the reason for the rejection
195	B. The ERO only needs to provide the taxpayer an explanation of the rejection
196	C. To timely file the return, the taxpayer must file the paper return by the later of the due date of the
197	return or ten calendar days after the notification of rejection
198	D. Taxpayers do not need to include any explanation with the paper return, as to why it is being filed
199	after the due date
200	Key: C
201	References: Notice 2010-13; Publication 1345, Handbook for Authorized e-file Providers of Individual
202	Income Tax Returns
